  "name": "Hepatitis A virus - IgG",
  "code": "HAV IGG",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">Hepatitis A virus (HAV) is endemic throughout the world, occurring most commonly, however, in areas of poor hygiene and low socioeconomic conditions. The virus is transmitted primarily by the fecal-oral route, and it is spread by close person-to-person contact and by food- and water-borne epidemics. Outbreaks frequently occur in overcrowded situations and in high-density institutions and centers, such as prisons and health care or daycare centers. Viral spread by parenteral routes (eg, exposure to blood) is possible but rare because infected individuals are viremic for a short period of time (usually &lt;3 weeks). There is little or no evidence of transplacental transmission from mother to fetus or transmission to newborn during delivery.</span><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">A negative result indicates that the patient has not been infected by HAV.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">Any patient showing an equivocal result should be retested on a second sample taken 1-2 weeks after the initial sample.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">A positive result is indicative of past or recent HAV infection.</span></p>",
